module

public import Cslib.Languages.LambdaCalculus.Named.Untyped.AlphaEquivProperties

/-! # Variants of α-equivalence

Five definitions of α-equivalence from [Crole2012], each capturing the same equivalence
relation on expressions:

* `∼p`  (Definition 3.1): Permutation-based with non-occurrence side condition (`AlphaEquiv`)
* `∼p#` (Definition 3.2): Permutation-based with freshness side condition (`AlphaEquivPFresh`)
* `∼¹p` (Definition 3.3): Permutation-based with non-occurrence on bodies only (`AlphaEquivP1`)
* `∼r`  (Definition 3.4): Traditional renaming axiom with non-occurrence (`AlphaEquivR`)
* `∼r#` (Definition 3.5): Renaming axiom with freshness (`AlphaEquivRFresh`)

All five say that two terms are α-equivalent when they differ only in the names of their bound
atoms. They differ in how a bound atom is renamed, and in how new the new name must be: either
it must not occur in the term at all, or it must only be *fresh*, that is, not occur free.

* `∼p`, `∼p#` and `∼¹p` compare two abstractions by renaming both bound atoms to the same new
  atom, by swapping as in [Gabbay2002], and comparing the bodies. `∼p` asks the most of the new
  atom. `∼p#` and `∼¹p` each weaken this side condition, in two different ways.
* `∼r` and `∼r#` follow the textbook definition: they are generated by renaming the bound atom
  of a single abstraction, `λx. m ∼ λx'. m[x := x']`. `∼r` requires the new atom not to occur
  in the abstraction; `∼r#` weakens this to freshness.

A weaker side condition can only relate more terms, so `∼p ⊆ ∼p#`, `∼p ⊆ ∼¹p` and `∼r ⊆ ∼r#`
are clear. That all five coincide is Section 4 of [Crole2012]. This file proves Theorem 4.1 and
states the remaining equalities as `theorem_wanted`.

## Main results

* `alphaEquiv_iff_alphaEquivPFresh`: Theorem 4.1 of [Crole2012], `∼p = ∼p#`
* `alphaEquiv_iff_alphaEquivP1`: Theorem 4.2 of [Crole2012], `∼p = ∼¹p` (wanted)
* `alphaEquiv_iff_alphaEquivR`: Theorem 4.4 of [Crole2012], `∼p = ∼r` (wanted)
* `alphaEquiv_iff_alphaEquivRFresh`: Theorem 4.5 of [Crole2012], `∼p = ∼r#` (wanted)
* `alphaEquivR_iff_alphaEquivRFresh`: Theorem 4.6 of [Crole2012], `∼r = ∼r#` (wanted)

## References

* [Roy L. Crole, *Alpha equivalence equalities*][Crole2012]
* [M. Gabbay and A. Pitts, *A New Approach to Abstract Syntax with Variable Binding*][Gabbay2002]

## Notation

Following the paper [Crole2012], we use the following correspondence between the paper's
abstract syntax and the λ-calculus terms:

| Paper         | Lean                |
|---------------|---------------------|
| `a`           | `Term.var x`        |
| `P(E₁, E₂)`   | `Term.app m1 m2`    |
| `B([a]E)`     | `Term.abs x m`      |
| `(z a) · E`   | `m.swap x z`        |
| `E{a'/a}`     | `m.subst a (var a')`|
| `π · E`       | `m.permute π`       |
-/

@[expose] public section

namespace Cslib

universe u

variable {Var : Type u} [DecidableEq Var] [HasFresh Var]

namespace LambdaCalculus.Named.Untyped.Term

/-- **Definition 3.2** [Crole2012]: `∼p#`, α-equivalence by swapping with a fresh atom.

`abs x1 m1` and `abs x2 m2` are related when their bodies agree after renaming `x1` and `x2` to a
common new atom. This weakens the side condition of `∼p` (`AlphaEquiv`): the new atom only has
to be fresh for the bodies, so it may occur bound in them. -/
inductive AlphaEquivPFresh : Term Var → Term Var → Prop where
  | var {x : Var} : AlphaEquivPFresh (var x) (var x)
  | abs {y x1 x2 : Var} {m1 m2 : Term Var} :
    y ∉ ({x1, x2} : Finset Var) ∪ m1.fv ∪ m2.fv →
    AlphaEquivPFresh (m1.swap x1 y) (m2.swap x2 y) →
    AlphaEquivPFresh (abs x1 m1) (abs x2 m2)
  | app {m1 n1 m2 n2 : Term Var} :
    AlphaEquivPFresh m1 n1 → AlphaEquivPFresh m2 n2 →
    AlphaEquivPFresh (app m1 m2) (app n1 n2)

/-- **Definition 3.3** [Crole2012]: `∼¹p`, α-equivalence by swapping, with no condition
relating the new atom to the binders.

`abs x1 m1` and `abs x2 m2` are related when their bodies agree after renaming `x1` and `x2` to a
common atom that occurs in neither body. This weakens the side condition of `∼p`
(`AlphaEquiv`) in a different way than `∼p#` does: the atom may be `x1` or `x2` itself. The
definition follows [Gabbay1999] (Theorem 2.1); the name refers to the variants of `∼p` in
Proposition 4.3 of [Crole2012]. -/
inductive AlphaEquivP1 : Term Var → Term Var → Prop where
  | var {x : Var} : AlphaEquivP1 (var x) (var x)
  | abs {y x1 x2 m1 m2} :
    y ∉ m1.vars ∪ m2.vars →
    AlphaEquivP1 (m1.rename x1 y) (m2.rename x2 y) →
    AlphaEquivP1 (abs x1 m1) (abs x2 m2)
  | app {m1 n1 m2 n2 : Term Var} :
    AlphaEquivP1 m1 n1 → AlphaEquivP1 m2 n2 →
    AlphaEquivP1 (app m1 m2) (app n1 n2)

/-- **Definition 3.4** [Crole2012]: `∼r`, the textbook α-equivalence.

The smallest congruence that allows renaming the bound atom of an abstraction to an atom that
does not occur in it: `abs x m` is related to `abs x' (m.subst x (var x'))`. This is the
definition of [Church1941] and [Barendregt1985] (Definition 2.1.11). -/
inductive AlphaEquivR : Term Var → Term Var → Prop where
  | refl {m : Term Var} : AlphaEquivR m m
  | symm {m1 m2 : Term Var} : AlphaEquivR m1 m2 → AlphaEquivR m2 m1
  | trans {m1 m2 m3 : Term Var} : AlphaEquivR m1 m2 → AlphaEquivR m2 m3 → AlphaEquivR m1 m3
  | app {m1 n1 m2 n2 : Term Var} :
    AlphaEquivR m1 n1 → AlphaEquivR m2 n2 →
    AlphaEquivR (app m1 m2) (app n1 n2)
  | abs_congr {x : Var} {m m' : Term Var} :
    AlphaEquivR m m' →
    AlphaEquivR (abs x m) (abs x m')
  | alpha {x x' : Var} {m : Term Var} :
    x' ∉ ({x} : Finset Var) ∪ m.vars →
    AlphaEquivR (abs x m) (abs x' (m.subst x (var x')))

/-- **Definition 3.5** [Crole2012]: `∼r#`, the textbook α-equivalence with a fresh atom.

This weakens the side condition of `∼r`: the new bound atom only has to be fresh for the
body, so it may occur bound in it, and the substitution renames those occurrences out of the
way. This is the
definition of [Hindley1988] (Section 1B, page 9). -/
inductive AlphaEquivRFresh : Term Var → Term Var → Prop where
  | refl {m : Term Var} : AlphaEquivRFresh m m
  | symm {m1 m2 : Term Var} :
    AlphaEquivRFresh m1 m2 → AlphaEquivRFresh m2 m1
  | trans {m1 m2 m3 : Term Var} :
    AlphaEquivRFresh m1 m2 → AlphaEquivRFresh m2 m3 →
    AlphaEquivRFresh m1 m3
  | app {m1 m1' m2 m2' : Term Var} :
    AlphaEquivRFresh m1 m1' → AlphaEquivRFresh m2 m2' →
    AlphaEquivRFresh (app m1 m2) (app m1' m2')
  | abs_congr {x : Var} {m m' : Term Var} :
    AlphaEquivRFresh m m' →
    AlphaEquivRFresh (abs x m) (abs x m')
  | alpha {x x' : Var} {m : Term Var} :
    x' ∉ ({x} : Finset Var) ∪ m.fv →
    AlphaEquivRFresh (abs x m) (abs x' (m.subst x (var x')))

omit [HasFresh Var] in
/-- Non-occurrence obviously implies freshness, and the `swap` operation coincides with
`rename` when the target variable does not occur in the term. -/
lemma alphaEquivPFresh_of_alphaEquiv {m n : Term Var} (h : m =α n) : AlphaEquivPFresh m n := by
  induction h with
  | var => exact .var
  | @abs z x1 x2 m1 m2 hz _ ih =>
    have h1 : z ∉ ({x1, x2} : Finset Var) ∪ m1.fv ∪ m2.fv := by
      grind [vars_eq_fv_union_bv]
    have h2 : AlphaEquivPFresh (m1.swap x1 z) (m2.swap x2 z) := by
      grind [swap_eq_rename_of_notMem_vars]
    exact AlphaEquivPFresh.abs h1 h2
  | app h1 h2 ih1 ih2 => exact AlphaEquivPFresh.app ih1 ih2

/-- The converse direction: each abstraction is α-equivalent to its body swapped to the fresh
witness `u` of the rule `pi#`, by Lemma 6.2 part 2 [Crole2012]. -/
lemma alphaEquiv_of_alphaEquivPFresh {m n : Term Var} (h : AlphaEquivPFresh m n) : m =α n := by
  induction h with
  | var => exact .var
  | @abs u a b m m' hy _ ih =>
    calc
      _ =α ((m.swap a u).abs u) := AlphaEquiv.abs_swap (by grind)
      _ =α ((m'.swap b u).abs u) := ih.abs_congr
      _ =α _ := (AlphaEquiv.abs_swap (by grind)).symm
  | app _ _ ih1 ih2 => exact AlphaEquiv.app ih1 ih2

/-- **Theorem 4.1** [Crole2012]: `∼p` and `∼p#` are the same relation. -/
theorem alphaEquiv_iff_alphaEquivPFresh (m n : Term Var) : m =α n ↔ AlphaEquivPFresh m n :=
  ⟨alphaEquivPFresh_of_alphaEquiv, alphaEquiv_of_alphaEquivPFresh⟩

/-- **Theorem 4.2** [Crole2012]: `∼p` and `∼¹p` are the same relation. -/
theorem_wanted alphaEquiv_iff_alphaEquivP1 (m n : Term Var) : m =α n ↔ AlphaEquivP1 m n

/-- **Theorem 4.4** [Crole2012]: `∼p` and `∼r` are the same relation. -/
theorem_wanted alphaEquiv_iff_alphaEquivR (m n : Term Var) : m =α n ↔ AlphaEquivR m n

/-- **Theorem 4.5** [Crole2012]: `∼p` and `∼r#` are the same relation. -/
theorem_wanted alphaEquiv_iff_alphaEquivRFresh (m n : Term Var) : m =α n ↔ AlphaEquivRFresh m n

/-- **Theorem 4.6** [Crole2012]: `∼r` and `∼r#` are the same relation. -/
theorem_wanted alphaEquivR_iff_alphaEquivRFresh (m n : Term Var) :
    AlphaEquivR m n ↔ AlphaEquivRFresh m n :=
  (❰alphaEquiv_iff_alphaEquivR❱ m n).symm.trans (❰alphaEquiv_iff_alphaEquivRFresh❱ m n)

end LambdaCalculus.Named.Untyped.Term

end Cslib
